  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The predictive performance relies on the density and distribution of commercial POIs, which may vary in accuracy or coverage between highly formal and informal urban areas.",
      "what": "This study predicts household income across 26,625 census sectors in Sao Paulo, Brazil, using crowd-sourced Google Maps Points of Interest (POIs). The researchers extracted POI counts, reduced their dimensionality using principal component analysis and non-negative matrix factorization, and trained gradient boosting regression models. The best-performing model achieved a held-out R² of 0.65 under a spatial validation design that prevents data leakage.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that commercial, crowd-sourced geospatial data can serve as a reliable, high-frequency proxy for sub-municipal income. This offers a low-cost method to update socioeconomic indicators during the long intercensal periods common in middle-income countries."
    }
